WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.255 --> 00:00:07.882
And so the enemy pilot dies the death

00:00:07.882 --> 00:00:10.010
that his stupidity deserved.

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:14.848
If you must charge towards an
aircraft, do it at an angle.

00:00:14.848 --> 00:00:16.808
Greetings all, I'm Nicholas Moran.

00:00:16.808 --> 00:00:19.602
I commissioned as an
armor officer for M1 tanks

00:00:19.602 --> 00:00:21.688
and then converted to M3 Bradleys.

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:24.357
These days, I work as a
civilian vehicle historian,

00:00:24.357 --> 00:00:26.151
primarily for World of Tanks.

00:00:26.151 --> 00:00:28.236
I'm back again to rate tank battles

00:00:28.236 --> 00:00:31.990
on movies and TV and
judge how real they are.

00:00:37.454 --> 00:00:42.083
The Ripsaw, which was
basically a tracked toy

00:00:42.083 --> 00:00:43.960
for people to drive around in,

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:45.795
looks just like it does in the movie here.

00:00:45.795 --> 00:00:47.505
It's because it had such lightweight

00:00:47.505 --> 00:00:49.215
and such a high-powered weight ratio,

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:52.093
the thing is incredibly
fast and incredibly nimble,

00:00:52.093 --> 00:00:54.054
which of course, attracts the
attention of the military,

00:00:54.054 --> 00:00:54.971
well, hang on a second.

00:00:54.971 --> 00:00:56.473
We've got this vehicle
that can go anywhere

00:00:56.473 --> 00:00:58.641
really, really quickly. Can
we do something with it?

00:00:58.641 --> 00:01:01.853
But the variant that the
Army has been looking at

00:01:01.853 --> 00:01:04.814
of late is actually a
robotic combat vehicle.

00:01:04.814 --> 00:01:08.193
I believe it's the RCVM,
and it's being plugged

00:01:08.193 --> 00:01:10.695
as the M5, and on the top,

00:01:10.695 --> 00:01:14.991
much like is shown in the clip,
is a remote weapon station.

00:01:19.621 --> 00:01:24.084
He's driving on ice, and those
don't look like ice tracks.

00:01:24.084 --> 00:01:25.752
Believe it or not, there's
actually ways you can get

00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:28.296
more grip on a tracked vehicle on ice.

00:01:28.296 --> 00:01:32.717
It's anything from snow chains,
those used in World War II,

00:01:32.717 --> 00:01:35.553
to these days, you can have grousers

00:01:35.553 --> 00:01:37.263
that you knock out a few track pads,

00:01:37.263 --> 00:01:41.684
and you put basically
cleats into the track.

00:01:41.684 --> 00:01:45.230
This thing is going quite fast
doing its various maneuvers

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:49.526
on ice, and that's
questionable in this context.

00:01:58.201 --> 00:02:02.872
I can think of no reason
except for an accession to plot

00:02:02.872 --> 00:02:07.377
why a tank would have a
grappling hook attached to it.

00:02:07.377 --> 00:02:09.754
The Lambo at the point
that they connect is

00:02:09.754 --> 00:02:12.465
basically underwater, and
that provides more drag

00:02:12.465 --> 00:02:14.843
than this thing can haul.

00:02:14.843 --> 00:02:16.136
Well, that's your point for realism.

00:02:16.136 --> 00:02:19.139
The door came off instead of
the Lambo getting pulled out.

00:02:19.139 --> 00:02:19.973
I'm going to be generous.

00:02:19.973 --> 00:02:21.391
I'm going to give it one
point because it has tracks.

00:02:21.391 --> 00:02:23.017
And I'm going to give it one point

00:02:23.017 --> 00:02:25.353
because it pulled the door
instead of the whole Lambo,

00:02:25.353 --> 00:02:27.272
so I'm going to give it a two.

00:02:31.192 --> 00:02:34.904
So you will have seen the
Americans had somebody standing

00:02:34.904 --> 00:02:37.073
on the back firing the caliber .50.

00:02:37.073 --> 00:02:40.493
The caliber .50 was originally
put onto American vehicles

00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:41.744
as an anti-aircraft weapon.

00:02:41.744 --> 00:02:44.372
And the reason that it's
on that external mount

00:02:44.372 --> 00:02:48.877
at the back is that it's a
lot easier to aim upwards

00:02:48.877 --> 00:02:52.005
if you're standing on the
back deck and then aiming up,

00:02:52.005 --> 00:02:55.258
as opposed to trying to stick
your head out of the hatch

00:02:55.258 --> 00:02:56.926
and maybe put it down from the side

00:02:56.926 --> 00:02:58.845
and you're not even
able to use the sights.

00:02:58.845 --> 00:03:00.096
Shermans!

00:03:06.811 --> 00:03:10.982
One of the things Sherman was
very good at was showing up

00:03:10.982 --> 00:03:12.275
when you needed a tank,

00:03:12.275 --> 00:03:14.652
and they tended not to go
around in ones and twos.

00:03:14.652 --> 00:03:17.947
They tended to go around in fives and 17s.

00:03:17.947 --> 00:03:19.574
Yeah, sure, on a tank-on-tank duel,

00:03:19.574 --> 00:03:21.826
it wasn't necessarily the best.

00:03:21.826 --> 00:03:25.538
For an infantryman that's
under heavy attack,

00:03:25.538 --> 00:03:29.250
he doesn't care if the best
tank in the world shows up,

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:33.338
as long as the tank which
shows up a) actually arrives,

00:03:33.338 --> 00:03:35.381
and b) is good enough to deal

00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:38.259
with whatever the
infantryman's problem is.

00:03:45.308 --> 00:03:47.769
So you'll notice that the
Germans don't actually have

00:03:47.769 --> 00:03:50.980
any tanks, which was a
problem that the Germans had

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:53.566
in World War II, is that their
industry simply wasn't up

00:03:53.566 --> 00:03:57.654
to the levels of American or Russian.

00:03:57.654 --> 00:03:59.322
So what we have here are a lot

00:03:59.322 --> 00:04:01.616
of assault guns and tank destroyers.

00:04:01.616 --> 00:04:03.493
It looks like a Marder there,
or at least a representation

00:04:03.493 --> 00:04:06.579
of one, a Sturmgeschütz,
which is an assault gun,

00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:08.456
and a Jagdpanther.

00:04:08.456 --> 00:04:12.460
The Jagdpanther is the only
one that is the serious threat

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:14.087
to an allied vehicle.

00:04:22.345 --> 00:04:25.265
Why didn't the Jagdpanther
just sit there and place

00:04:25.265 --> 00:04:27.725
its extremely tough frontal
armor to the Shermans?

00:04:27.725 --> 00:04:28.559
Well, you could do that,

00:04:28.559 --> 00:04:32.230
but then you were probably now
placing your weak flank armor

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:34.065
to the American infantry, who are equipped

00:04:34.065 --> 00:04:36.985
in the scene with bazookas,
anti-tank rocket launchers.

00:04:36.985 --> 00:04:39.696
The Germans now are flanked,
and so very sensibly,

00:04:39.696 --> 00:04:43.032
they immediately abandon the
attack and start falling back.

00:04:43.032 --> 00:04:44.826
This is as good as you're going to get

00:04:44.826 --> 00:04:46.327
on celluloid or on digits.

00:04:46.327 --> 00:04:47.954
If there is ever going to be a 10

00:04:47.954 --> 00:04:50.707
for "rate a tank battle,"
this is probably it.

00:04:58.506 --> 00:05:00.883
The tanks represent Saint-Chamond.

00:05:00.883 --> 00:05:03.136
There's only one that exists in the world.

00:05:03.136 --> 00:05:05.805
It's at the French national
tank museum in Saumur.

00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:07.932
Now, the cognoscenti will look
at the road wheels on that

00:05:07.932 --> 00:05:09.642
and realize that the vehicle is based off

00:05:09.642 --> 00:05:13.062
a BMP or an MT-LB, which are
Soviet Cold War vehicles.

00:05:16.649 --> 00:05:18.985
By this point in the war, we're talking

00:05:18.985 --> 00:05:22.238
towards the end of 1918, the
Germans are very familiar

00:05:22.238 --> 00:05:24.699
with tanks, but you'll still
want to shoot them anyway

00:05:24.699 --> 00:05:25.533
for a couple of reasons.

00:05:25.533 --> 00:05:28.494
One is if the tanks are
accompanying infantry,

00:05:28.494 --> 00:05:30.204
well, you're going to have
an effect on the infantry

00:05:30.204 --> 00:05:31.873
and maybe suppress them to the point

00:05:31.873 --> 00:05:33.791
that the tanks are going
to advance on their own.

00:05:33.791 --> 00:05:38.004
Secondly, the tanks were not
the most robust at the time.

00:05:38.004 --> 00:05:41.507
There was a problem known
as splash, especially when

00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:44.385
once the tankers would actually
wear a type of chain mail

00:05:44.385 --> 00:05:48.556
in front of their face with
goggles to stop the pieces

00:05:48.556 --> 00:05:51.434
of bullet coming in the gaps in the armor.

00:05:51.434 --> 00:05:53.061
Finally, it's psychological.

00:05:53.061 --> 00:05:56.397
It makes you feel a lot better
to actually do something,

00:05:56.397 --> 00:05:58.649
even if it's a gesture of futility.

00:06:02.737 --> 00:06:05.948
The early tank guns for
World War I would have

00:06:05.948 --> 00:06:08.701
generally either been
converted artillery pieces

00:06:08.701 --> 00:06:10.453
or, quite often, naval guns.

00:06:10.453 --> 00:06:13.206
The big problem you're going to have

00:06:13.206 --> 00:06:15.249
with getting them close
is called depression.

00:06:15.249 --> 00:06:19.003
So there is a stop as to how
far down the gun will depress,

00:06:19.003 --> 00:06:21.631
and that determines how close you can get

00:06:21.631 --> 00:06:25.593
to the enemy trench before you
can have to stop engaging it.

00:06:29.097 --> 00:06:32.767
To this date, trench-crossing
ability in terms of meters,

00:06:32.767 --> 00:06:34.811
how many meters of
trench you can cross, is

00:06:34.811 --> 00:06:36.646
still a criterion.

00:06:36.646 --> 00:06:40.108
The idea of can you cross
it without collapsing it,

00:06:40.108 --> 00:06:42.735
well, that depends if you
want to collapse it or not.

00:06:42.735 --> 00:06:46.322
So even as late as the Iraq campaigns,

00:06:46.322 --> 00:06:48.783
a tank would go up to
fortified enemy position,

00:06:48.783 --> 00:06:52.745
turn on the spot, and
bury the people inside.

00:06:52.745 --> 00:06:56.290
And it's one of those unfortunate
realities of basically,

00:06:56.290 --> 00:06:58.418
tanks have many ways of killing you,

00:06:58.418 --> 00:07:00.128
and the tracks are one of them.

00:07:04.715 --> 00:07:07.218
You'd think today a hand grenade
isn't going to do very much.

00:07:07.218 --> 00:07:09.137
The tracks on the
Saint-Chamond were basically

00:07:09.137 --> 00:07:13.015
for my whole tractor, so
they weren't as battle-proof

00:07:13.015 --> 00:07:15.726
as a modern vehicle would be.

00:07:15.726 --> 00:07:17.353
In fact, the armor on
this is so thin that a lot

00:07:17.353 --> 00:07:21.107
of heavy bullets, they even
developed rifle bullets

00:07:21.107 --> 00:07:23.317
with an armor-piercing core could go

00:07:23.317 --> 00:07:24.944
through these early tanks.

00:07:24.944 --> 00:07:27.405
This is an eight and a half, maybe nine.

00:07:27.405 --> 00:07:29.532
I could quibble a few things,

00:07:29.532 --> 00:07:31.993
but it generally gets the concept across.

00:07:38.708 --> 00:07:40.918
Oh, goody.

00:07:40.918 --> 00:07:45.923
So Rambo has gotten into his,
I'm going to call it a T-72.

00:07:46.048 --> 00:07:47.925
It's not actually,

00:07:47.925 --> 00:07:50.470
but the helicopter here represents a Hind.

00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:53.473
I think it's actually
a Puma, but the Hind,

00:07:53.473 --> 00:07:58.186
the Mi-24, was a gunship
that still actually has

00:07:58.186 --> 00:08:01.230
a troop-carrying capacity,
as well as carrying a lot

00:08:01.230 --> 00:08:04.609
of rockets and missiles
and being heavily armored.

00:08:04.609 --> 00:08:08.029
It's basically a flying tank,
for lack of a better term.

00:08:14.619 --> 00:08:16.871
So hang on, is he
driving, or is he gunning?

00:08:16.871 --> 00:08:18.539
There's only one tank that I can think of

00:08:18.539 --> 00:08:21.918
that is fully combat-capable
with one crewman,

00:08:21.918 --> 00:08:24.420
and that is the Swedish Strv 103,

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:26.255
commonly known as the S-tank.

00:08:26.255 --> 00:08:29.342
The driver functions
as the gunner as well,

00:08:29.342 --> 00:08:30.885
and this is why you're supposed to go

00:08:30.885 --> 00:08:34.013
with anti-aircraft vehicles
in company with you,

00:08:34.013 --> 00:08:37.767
because their job is to defend
you against air threats.

00:08:44.982 --> 00:08:48.069
So our enemy is in an attack
helicopter, a gunship,

00:08:48.069 --> 00:08:51.739
and it's got missiles on the
tips, AT-2 Swatters probably,

00:08:51.739 --> 00:08:54.116
that are effective from
several kilometers away.

00:08:54.116 --> 00:08:57.203
And instead, he's firing unguided rockets

00:08:57.203 --> 00:09:01.582
at the front of the Rambo tank.

00:09:01.582 --> 00:09:03.918
Why doesn't he just fly
around the back and shoot

00:09:03.918 --> 00:09:06.796
the rockets into the engine
deck or the rear turret,

00:09:06.796 --> 00:09:09.465
or you know, some place
that'll actually cause damage?

00:09:16.806 --> 00:09:19.392
And so the enemy pilot dies the death

00:09:19.392 --> 00:09:21.686
that his stupidity deserved.

00:09:21.686 --> 00:09:25.481
Officially, if you must
charge towards an aircraft,

00:09:25.481 --> 00:09:27.108
do it at an angle.

00:09:27.108 --> 00:09:30.736
He has to not only aim for lead,

00:09:30.736 --> 00:09:33.906
but also the change in
distance at the same time.

00:09:33.906 --> 00:09:35.741
Whereas if you're
charging straight at him,

00:09:35.741 --> 00:09:37.493
you're simplifying the problem

00:09:37.493 --> 00:09:38.953
for the aircraft that's coming at you.

00:09:38.953 --> 00:09:40.621
He doesn't need to worry about aiming.

00:09:40.621 --> 00:09:42.582
It has a tank, so I'll give it a one.

00:09:45.251 --> 00:09:46.085
Fire.

00:09:49.046 --> 00:09:50.715
The premise of an ambush

00:09:50.715 --> 00:09:54.635
in an urban setting by
armor is not unheard of,

00:09:54.635 --> 00:09:57.888
but the idea of armor
operating in an urban setting

00:09:57.888 --> 00:10:00.975
without infantry support
is normally questionable.

00:10:00.975 --> 00:10:05.021
Panthers are not well suited
for close-range fighting.

00:10:05.021 --> 00:10:07.815
They really would excel
on the Eastern Front,

00:10:07.815 --> 00:10:09.775
well, the steppes of Ukraine, for example,

00:10:09.775 --> 00:10:11.652
nice long lines of fire.

00:10:11.652 --> 00:10:14.447
That said, they can get
away with it in this context

00:10:14.447 --> 00:10:17.992
because they know that
the enemy is a single tank

00:10:17.992 --> 00:10:19.160
with no infantry.

00:10:21.954 --> 00:10:23.205
Oh look, you're going
through a building again,

00:10:23.205 --> 00:10:24.665
except this time he's
done one thing right.

00:10:24.665 --> 00:10:27.001
He's got the turret
traversed over the rear.

00:10:27.001 --> 00:10:28.544
I'd mentioned before, you don't want to go

00:10:28.544 --> 00:10:30.129
through a building if you can avoid it.

00:10:30.129 --> 00:10:31.380
In this case, I guess they couldn't,

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:32.673
because they're getting shot at.

00:10:32.673 --> 00:10:35.593
And if you are going to go
through, spin your turret over,

00:10:35.593 --> 00:10:37.803
and you'll keep yourself safe.

00:10:45.311 --> 00:10:46.145
And what you can see here,

00:10:46.145 --> 00:10:49.231
the T-34-85 has a very
neat traverse handle,

00:10:49.231 --> 00:10:50.483
so it performs two functions.

00:10:50.483 --> 00:10:53.277
In this case, you can see
he has it in manual mode,

00:10:53.277 --> 00:10:55.404
cranking around, as
opposed to electric mode,

00:10:55.404 --> 00:10:57.615
which, you simply flip
the handle 90 degrees.

00:10:57.615 --> 00:11:00.576
However, maybe the gunner
just doesn't know this,

00:11:00.576 --> 00:11:04.080
because in the movie,
this was a found tank.

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:06.916
Even myself, when I got into
a T-34-85 for the first time,

00:11:06.916 --> 00:11:09.418
and trying to see where is
the second control handle,

00:11:09.418 --> 00:11:10.586
I can't find it.

00:11:19.887 --> 00:11:21.764
This is quite feasible.

00:11:21.764 --> 00:11:23.516
When faced with the Panther
in this close range,

00:11:23.516 --> 00:11:25.476
T-34 crew now has a choice.

00:11:25.476 --> 00:11:29.897
It can either run away, or
it can attempt to engage.

00:11:29.897 --> 00:11:31.857
Now, I would probably agree

00:11:31.857 --> 00:11:33.776
with the 34 crew on this for two reasons.

00:11:33.776 --> 00:11:36.195
One, the Panther was looking
the wrong way entirely.

00:11:36.195 --> 00:11:40.449
And the other thing is Panthers
are not the easiest thing

00:11:40.449 --> 00:11:41.283
in the world to kill,

00:11:41.283 --> 00:11:43.327
unless you can get around
the side or behind them.

00:11:43.327 --> 00:11:46.414
And in this case, they're
already behind them,

00:11:46.414 --> 00:11:47.665
so why not engage?

00:11:47.665 --> 00:11:50.918
But the only thing I might have
done differently was back up

00:11:50.918 --> 00:11:52.837
two or three tank lengths

00:11:52.837 --> 00:11:55.923
and aim the gun into the engine deck.

00:11:55.923 --> 00:11:58.843
That would be a more guaranteed kill

00:11:58.843 --> 00:12:00.553
than trying to aim for the turret ring.

00:12:08.144 --> 00:12:10.146
So the explosion was
actually a little bit faster

00:12:10.146 --> 00:12:11.439
than what would really happen.

00:12:11.439 --> 00:12:14.442
Generally speaking, when a
tank is hit, it takes a couple

00:12:14.442 --> 00:12:17.653
of seconds, and those are
your couple of seconds

00:12:17.653 --> 00:12:19.780
that is the difference
between life and death

00:12:19.780 --> 00:12:21.157
when you're getting out of a vehicle.

00:12:21.157 --> 00:12:25.202
Although in reality, the
net-effect difference

00:12:25.202 --> 00:12:27.955
to the crew is academic.

00:12:27.955 --> 00:12:29.623
They're basically toast.

00:12:29.623 --> 00:12:32.668
In this context, I don't
know, maybe a seven.

00:12:38.382 --> 00:12:42.428
The movie is set about the
time of the Yemeni civil war.

00:12:42.428 --> 00:12:44.263
It's actually kind of still ongoing.

00:12:44.263 --> 00:12:46.599
The film was made in Morocco,

00:12:46.599 --> 00:12:50.811
hence, you have the M60s and
the T-72s. So the exact nature

00:12:50.811 --> 00:12:53.856
of the tanks that you get in
Yemen isn't entirely there,

00:12:53.856 --> 00:12:57.526
but hey, for movie purposes,
it gets the point across.

00:12:57.526 --> 00:13:03.199
Certainly there are Western
and Eastern tanks in the area.

00:13:03.199 --> 00:13:05.743
Tank Alpha, attack from the right!

00:13:06.535 --> 00:13:07.620
Hong Xu!

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:10.164
Turn 20 degrees left, hurry!

00:13:10.790 --> 00:13:11.999
So what I have here is

00:13:11.999 --> 00:13:15.044
a VIC-1 CVC, combat
vehicle crewman, helmet.

00:13:15.044 --> 00:13:16.545
And the reason you would
want to wear a helmet

00:13:16.545 --> 00:13:18.923
even if you're inside
the tank is quite simply,

00:13:18.923 --> 00:13:19.840
you're being thrown around,

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:23.385
and there's a lot of sharp,
pointy things sticking out at you

00:13:23.385 --> 00:13:24.470
that as you're being thrown around,

00:13:24.470 --> 00:13:25.513
you don't want to hurt your head.

00:13:25.513 --> 00:13:28.516
And of course, you also
have the dust goggles,

00:13:29.725 --> 00:13:32.102
because the tanks are throwing
a lot of dust up at you.

00:13:32.102 --> 00:13:33.103
You don't have a windshield.

00:13:33.103 --> 00:13:34.396
If you're doing 40 miles an hour,

00:13:34.396 --> 00:13:36.315
there's still enough
wind that you'll notice,

00:13:36.315 --> 00:13:38.275
so you wear a set of goggles, as well.

00:13:42.488 --> 00:13:44.406
It's good to see armor actually work.

00:13:44.406 --> 00:13:46.826
So what has happened there is a round hit

00:13:46.826 --> 00:13:49.787
explosive reactive armor. So
those bricks that you see,

00:13:49.787 --> 00:13:52.164
and what they generally consist of,

00:13:52.164 --> 00:13:54.583
though this is a gross oversimplification,

00:13:54.583 --> 00:13:56.794
is a couple of pieces of metal sandwiching

00:13:56.794 --> 00:13:59.588
a piece of explosive, and
that when a round hits it,

00:13:59.588 --> 00:14:03.342
it explodes and disrupt or destabilize it

00:14:03.342 --> 00:14:08.180
to the extent that the main
armor belt behind can stop it.

00:14:08.180 --> 00:14:09.557
Turn right!

00:14:11.433 --> 00:14:12.810
Nan Xia, get ready!

00:14:13.602 --> 00:14:14.979
Fire!

00:14:17.439 --> 00:14:18.607
You got a couple of problems.

00:14:18.607 --> 00:14:22.570
Firstly, if the entire
turret system is off line,

00:14:22.570 --> 00:14:24.405
then the tank is still
rocking a little bit.

00:14:24.405 --> 00:14:26.615
It may not look it much, but
it's going to be rocking enough

00:14:26.615 --> 00:14:31.245
that he cannot hip-shoot
a 125 on a point target

00:14:31.245 --> 00:14:32.746
like a tank like that.

00:14:32.746 --> 00:14:35.124
Secondly, he literally is hip-shooting,

00:14:35.124 --> 00:14:36.333
and he's eyeballing it.

00:14:36.333 --> 00:14:38.961
I don't see why he couldn't
still be using the gunsight.

00:14:38.961 --> 00:14:41.130
He can then pull the trigger himself.

00:14:49.597 --> 00:14:50.848
Sandstorms do happen.

00:14:50.848 --> 00:14:53.726
In the 2003 invasion
of Iraq, there was one

00:14:53.726 --> 00:14:55.936
that basically shut down the
war for a couple of days.

00:14:55.936 --> 00:14:58.898
Well, your big problem with a
sandstorm is the air filters.

00:14:58.898 --> 00:15:01.066
So the sand is going to
get into your air filters

00:15:01.066 --> 00:15:02.443
and clog them up very quickly.

00:15:02.443 --> 00:15:04.445
However, in this context,
I don't think they had

00:15:04.445 --> 00:15:06.113
a heck of a lot of choice,
because being chased

00:15:06.113 --> 00:15:07.865
by a platoon of M60s,

00:15:07.865 --> 00:15:09.617
they were probably going to die anyway,

00:15:09.617 --> 00:15:13.787
so why not make for the
sandstorm and hope for the best?

00:15:13.787 --> 00:15:16.206
So I think seven's probably fair.

00:15:21.295 --> 00:15:23.130
I'm not even sure where to start.

00:15:23.130 --> 00:15:28.135
I'm trying to think of any
reasonable military context

00:15:29.261 --> 00:15:31.138
that this vehicle could exist in.

00:15:31.138 --> 00:15:36.143
The US Army has a vehicle, the
M1117, which is a derivative

00:15:36.185 --> 00:15:38.228
of the old LAV-100s from Vietnam.

00:15:38.228 --> 00:15:40.564
It's basically a huge, armored 4-by-4

00:15:40.564 --> 00:15:44.610
with a gun turret on the
top for military police use.

00:15:44.610 --> 00:15:46.862
I'm sure they wouldn't
mind something like this.

00:15:50.908 --> 00:15:54.370
So generally speaking, convoys,
just because of the nature

00:15:54.370 --> 00:15:57.998
of roads, you tend to be in
column instead of line abreast

00:15:57.998 --> 00:15:59.208
like in the shot here.

00:15:59.208 --> 00:16:01.961
You have a bit of a choice,
depending on how many vehicles

00:16:01.961 --> 00:16:04.838
you have, is how far apart
you want the vehicles to be.

00:16:04.838 --> 00:16:07.716
Because the idea is that
your lead vehicle is going

00:16:07.716 --> 00:16:11.136
to be sufficiently far in
front of whatever it is

00:16:11.136 --> 00:16:13.180
that you're protecting the
main body of the convoy.

00:16:13.180 --> 00:16:16.725
So with only three vehicles,
you're kind of limited

00:16:16.725 --> 00:16:20.562
as to what you can do, but
your typical is going to be

00:16:20.562 --> 00:16:23.190
maybe two up front, then the main asset,

00:16:23.190 --> 00:16:24.775
and then one bringing up the rear.

00:16:30.739 --> 00:16:33.742
Cannons are not common,
but they are a thing.

00:16:33.742 --> 00:16:36.495
You can sometimes find
them mounted as experiments

00:16:36.495 --> 00:16:39.623
on tanks as well, but
they're basically a waste

00:16:39.623 --> 00:16:42.001
of space and weight, so you
don't see them on tanks.

00:16:46.088 --> 00:16:48.298
In terms of a missile launcher,

00:16:48.298 --> 00:16:51.635
yeah, missile launchers on
armored cars are a thing.

00:16:51.635 --> 00:16:55.014
This particular elevating
out of the 4-by-4 is

00:16:55.014 --> 00:16:59.518
probably closer to a
BRDM anti-tank system,

00:16:59.518 --> 00:17:01.979
but that's actually for
anti-armor purposes,

00:17:01.979 --> 00:17:03.981
not anti-aircraft.

00:17:03.981 --> 00:17:06.483
But hey, I guess when
you're dealing with Batman,

00:17:06.483 --> 00:17:07.818
anything goes.

00:17:07.818 --> 00:17:11.989
I got nothing. Zero. Sorry. No.

00:17:19.371 --> 00:17:21.790
The Germans appear to be
equipped with Pak 40s.

00:17:21.790 --> 00:17:26.128
It was a very good anti-tank
gun, 7.5 cm caliber,

00:17:26.128 --> 00:17:29.840
definitely a valid threat against an M4.

00:17:29.840 --> 00:17:31.800
Anti-tank! H.E.! Traverse left.

00:17:33.761 --> 00:17:35.971
Traverse left!

00:17:35.971 --> 00:17:38.682
That was a doctrinal fire command.

00:17:38.682 --> 00:17:40.559
Now, the reason I know this
is they actually sent me

00:17:40.559 --> 00:17:44.021
the script for filming
it. But that is basically

00:17:44.021 --> 00:17:48.358
alert, target, and command of execution

00:17:48.358 --> 00:17:50.736
in abbreviated fire command.

00:17:50.736 --> 00:17:52.738
The fire commands back in
World War II weren't quite

00:17:52.738 --> 00:17:54.198
as regimented as they are today,

00:17:54.198 --> 00:17:56.241
but the fundamentals are
actually still the same.

00:18:00.079 --> 00:18:02.081
You have an argument.
Do you want to advance

00:18:02.081 --> 00:18:04.208
over open ground as a whole platoon,

00:18:04.208 --> 00:18:05.834
or do you want to bound in sections?

00:18:05.834 --> 00:18:07.544
In this case, because of
the amount of infantry

00:18:07.544 --> 00:18:09.213
that they're trying to provide cover for,

00:18:09.213 --> 00:18:14.051
I can see perhaps why they
went as a full platoon on line.

00:18:14.051 --> 00:18:16.512
But also, it's a very,
very open, long field,

00:18:16.512 --> 00:18:18.972
so maybe it was necessary
for the entire platoon

00:18:18.972 --> 00:18:22.518
to advance on line simply so
that they could see the target.

00:18:25.312 --> 00:18:28.482
Another gun, who's got eyes on it?

00:18:28.482 --> 00:18:31.151
Somebody's going to ask,
how can a Pak 40 miss

00:18:31.151 --> 00:18:32.986
at such short range?

00:18:32.986 --> 00:18:36.698
You'll be surprised what
stress is going to do to you.

00:18:36.698 --> 00:18:40.494
So there have been
studies, and the reputation

00:18:40.494 --> 00:18:42.788
of the 88 is such that it doesn't miss.

00:18:42.788 --> 00:18:46.875
In reality, they're firing 20
rounds to get a single kill,

00:18:46.875 --> 00:18:51.880
because the stress of combat
is such that it is not

00:18:52.548 --> 00:18:54.883
calm and collected on a gunnery range

00:18:54.883 --> 00:18:56.176
when you're doing scientific testing.

00:18:56.176 --> 00:18:58.720
It's, "Oh God, I'm about to die."

00:18:58.720 --> 00:19:02.808
Let's light them up. Let's
go. Let's clean it up.

00:19:06.311 --> 00:19:09.148
Now, that scene is basically
why I became a tanker.

00:19:09.148 --> 00:19:12.025
A line of tanks, every machine gun going.

00:19:12.025 --> 00:19:14.444
A single tank is considered
to have firepower

00:19:14.444 --> 00:19:17.156
more or less equivalent
to an infantry platoon,

00:19:17.156 --> 00:19:18.949
because you have the
heavy machine gun up top,

00:19:18.949 --> 00:19:21.368
you got a co-ax machine gun,
you got a bow machine gun,

00:19:21.368 --> 00:19:23.829
and oh, by the way, you've got a cannon.

00:19:23.829 --> 00:19:26.206
That is a lot of firepower
from a single vehicle,

00:19:26.206 --> 00:19:28.417
and you can imagine this sheer suppression

00:19:28.417 --> 00:19:30.335
and psychological effect
that comes of this,

00:19:30.335 --> 00:19:31.962
let alone the lethal effects.

00:19:31.962 --> 00:19:33.172
I know there were folks that didn't like

00:19:33.172 --> 00:19:35.465
that I gave the last "Fury" a nine,

00:19:35.465 --> 00:19:37.467
but I'm giving this one a nine as well.

00:19:41.388 --> 00:19:43.432
I can't get elevation and he knows it.

00:19:43.432 --> 00:19:46.643
So this was an actual
problem in Afghanistan,

00:19:46.643 --> 00:19:49.688
and it's a mountainous
country, is that their tanks,

00:19:49.688 --> 00:19:51.899
which were designed
really for open warfare

00:19:51.899 --> 00:19:55.027
on the plains of Germany,
can't elevate high enough.

00:19:55.027 --> 00:19:58.155
So what the Soviets ended
up doing was they developed

00:19:58.155 --> 00:20:01.825
a variant of the ZSU-23-4
anti-aircraft gun,

00:20:01.825 --> 00:20:04.786
the Shilka, called the Afghani.

00:20:04.786 --> 00:20:06.455
So they would use those in conjunction

00:20:06.455 --> 00:20:08.332
with the tanks to provide

00:20:08.332 --> 00:20:12.461
that close-range, high-elevation security

00:20:12.461 --> 00:20:15.964
that Daskal here is having a problem with.

00:20:21.428 --> 00:20:24.181
The actual effect of hitting
the gun tube is not going to be

00:20:24.181 --> 00:20:27.184
the petaling like you see
here. That's for visual effect.

00:20:27.184 --> 00:20:29.728
What you're actually going
to get is a very small hole

00:20:30.646 --> 00:20:32.105
that goes through the gun tube,

00:20:32.105 --> 00:20:33.315
but it's still plenty bad enough

00:20:33.315 --> 00:20:34.608
that you've disabled the gun.

00:20:41.865 --> 00:20:45.160
The grenade will not have
separated the tracks,

00:20:45.160 --> 00:20:47.621
so I must conclude it was the rocks,

00:20:47.621 --> 00:20:50.290
but it's not impossible for rocky terrain

00:20:50.290 --> 00:20:55.295
and maybe a less experienced
driver to angle his track

00:20:55.295 --> 00:20:59.174
in such a way when he's turning
that he gets rocks caught

00:20:59.174 --> 00:21:02.344
in between the wheels and the
tracks and snaps the track pin,

00:21:02.344 --> 00:21:04.304
and that throws your track and loses it.

00:21:04.304 --> 00:21:07.557
This scene is probably
a seven to an eight.

00:21:07.557 --> 00:21:09.518
So out of the clips that we saw today,

00:21:09.518 --> 00:21:12.479
if I had to recommend one
and one only to watch,

00:21:12.479 --> 00:21:14.439
I'd probably go with "Band of Brothers,"

00:21:14.439 --> 00:21:21.071
which is more of an infantry-related
show, but tanks work

00:21:21.071 --> 00:21:24.866
with the infantry, and it's
always good to understand it.

00:21:24.866 --> 00:21:25.867
Well, thanks for watching.

00:21:25.867 --> 00:21:27.911
I hope you found it
interesting and informative.

00:21:27.911 --> 00:21:30.080
If you want more the same sort of thing,

00:21:30.080 --> 00:21:31.540
add one form, traverse right,

00:21:31.540 --> 00:21:33.500
and click the video in that box.

